course: everyday
lesson: 87
title: Eyes, ears and moving your body
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your eyes and ears are how you learn about the world; if you cannot see the board or hear the teacher clearly, a simple test can find out why, and glasses or hearing aids can help.
explain: Inside the ear are tiny hair cells that turn sound into signals for the brain; very loud noise can damage them for ever, and ringing in the ears is a warning.
explain: Being active for at least an hour a day keeps your heart, muscles and mind strong.
explain: A boy in Bristol could not read the board, so he told his teacher; an eye test showed he needed glasses, and now his maths is much easier.
fact: If you are told you need glasses, wear them as the optician (the person who tests eyes and makes glasses) says, because they help you see clearly and learn better.
fact: If you cannot see the board at school, tell a teacher and a parent, because you may need an eye test.
fact: Clean glasses with water and a soft cloth, not your T-shirt, because rough cloth scratches the lenses.
fact: Many people wear glasses; if someone calls you names for wearing them, tell an adult.
fact: Wear ear protection near very loud noise like fireworks or concerts, because loud noise damages the inner ear.
fact: Ringing in the ears after loud noise is a warning of damage, so tell an adult and rest your ears.
fact: If you find it hard to hear the teacher, tell a parent, because you may need a hearing test.
fact: To help a deaf or hard-of-hearing classmate, face them and speak clearly without shouting, so they can lip-read.
fact: A hearing aid helps a person hear, so respect it and do not touch it.
fact: Children should be active for at least an hour a day.
fact: A stitch while running passes if you slow down and breathe deeply.
fact: For a sprained ankle, rest it, put a cold pack on, raise it and tell an adult.
fact: Studies have found no link between cracking knuckles and arthritis (stiff, painful joints).
exercise: The optician says you need glasses. What should you do? | Wear them as the optician says | Wear them only on your birthday | Keep them in your bag to stay safe | Wear a friend's glasses instead
exercise: You cannot read the words on the board. What should you do? | Tell a teacher and a parent | Copy from your neighbour forever | Squint and say nothing | Sit further back in the class
exercise: How should you clean your glasses? | With water and a soft cloth | With the bottom of your T-shirt | With sand to polish them | With a rough paper towel
exercise: A boy laughs at you for wearing glasses. What can you do? | Tell an adult and remember glasses are normal | Stop wearing your glasses, even if you cannot see well | Break his glasses in return | Hide in the toilets at break
exercise: You are going to a fireworks show. What protects your ears? | Ear protection like ear defenders | Standing closer to the fireworks so they seem quieter | Turning your head away quickly | Covering one eye
exercise: Your ears are ringing after a loud concert. What does it mean? | It is a warning of damage to your ears | Your ears are getting stronger and tougher | It is a sign of a cold coming | You have a very good sense of hearing
exercise: A new classmate is hard of hearing. How can you help? | Face her and speak clearly without shouting | Shout every word loudly right into her ear | Talk with your back to her | Write her notes in tiny letters
exercise: A classmate wears a hearing aid. What is right? | Respect it and do not touch it | Ask to try it on | Hide it as a joke | Tell him to take it off at break
exercise: You find it hard to hear the teacher in class. What should you do? | Tell a parent; you may need a hearing test | Pretend you heard everything | Ask the teacher to whisper | Ignore it, as it will soon pass by itself
exercise: How long should children be active each day? | At least an hour | About five minutes | Only at weekends | Ten hours without rest
exercise: You get a stitch while running. What helps? | Slow down and breathe deeply | Run faster to beat it | Hold your breath for a minute | Drink a big glass of juice
exercise: You twist your ankle and it swells. What do you do? | Rest, a cold pack, raise it and tell an adult | Keep running on it to loosen it up again | Put it in hot water for an hour | Rub it hard to push the swelling out
exercise: Which is true about cracking your knuckles? | No link to arthritis has been found | It always causes arthritis later in life | It makes your fingers longer | It breaks the bones in your hand
test: Why do glasses help a child do better at school? | They let the child see clearly | They make the child cleverer | They protect eyes from germs | They make the board come closer
test: A girl in Edinburgh hears a ringing sound after a loud party. What should she do? | Tell an adult and give her ears a rest | Turn her music up loud to cover the ringing | Put a cotton bud deep in her ears | Ignore it, it is nothing
test: Why should you not wipe glasses on your jumper? | Rough cloth scratches the lenses | It makes the jumper dirty and greasy | It makes the glasses magnetic | The lenses will melt
test: A boy in Wellington needs to talk to a deaf friend. What works best? | Facing him and speaking clearly | Talking very fast from behind | Shouting loudly in his ear | Whispering so others do not hear
test: Why is moving for an hour a day good for you? | It keeps your heart, muscles and mind strong | It makes sure you never get ill | It means you can eat only sweets | It makes you grow by a centimetre each day
test: A girl twisted her ankle at netball. Which plan helps the swelling go down? | Rest it, cool it and raise it | Walk on it a lot to stretch it | Heat it with a hot water bottle | Tie it very tightly with string
test: Why should children not stand next to the speakers at a concert? | Loud noise can damage the inner ear | Speakers can give an electric shock | The music sounds worse up close | Speakers are very hot
test: A friend says cracking your knuckles gives you arthritis. What is true? | Studies found no link, though the noise can annoy others | It is true and happens within a week | It is true only for children | It makes the knuckles stronger instead
test: cant read the board from the back, its all blurry | Tell a teacher and a parent, you may need an eye test | Squint harder every day | Copy from your friend instead | Just sit there and say nothing, it'll get better on its own
test: gonna wipe my glasses on my tshirt | Use water and a soft cloth, T-shirts scratch | That is the best way to clean | Lick them, then rub them | Use a paper towel and scrub really hard
test: ears ringing after the fireworks | It is a warning of damage, tell an adult and rest them | It is normal, ignore it | Turn up your music really loud to cover it up | Put cotton buds in deep
test: new girl's deaf, should i shout so she hears | Face her and speak clearly without shouting | Shout right in her ear so she can hear every word | Talk behind her back quickly | Write nothing and wave instead
explain: A girl in Ontario could not see the board for months but did not say; her marks dropped, and when she finally got glasses she said everything looked sharp for the first time.
explain: A boy in Bristol skipped warming up and his helmet at the skate park; he fell, hit his head and needed stitches above his eye.
fact: Children who cannot see well often do not know it, so eye tests at school or an optician matter.
fact: A helmet greatly lowers the chance of a serious head injury when cycling, skating or scootering.
test: can't read the board from the back, prob nothing | Tell a teacher or parent, you might need glasses | Squint harder every day, it trains the eyes to see better | Sit at the back so eyes work more | Only grown-ups need glasses
test: helmet makes my hair weird, skating without | Wear it, falls on hard ground can badly hurt your head | Skip it if you're already really good at skating n never fall | Helmets are only for races | Only wear it on hills
test: lasers r cool, pointing one at my mate's eyes | No, a laser can damage eyes for good | Yes, eyes blink fast enough to stay safe | Only the red ones hurt | It's safe if he wears glasses
test: Ben's telling everyone if u crack ur knuckles u get arthritis n ur fingers go all twisted by 20 | Studies found no link to arthritis, so that part is just a rumour | It is true, and it happens by the age of 20 | It is true, but only for the thumbs | It is true if you crack them more than once a day
test: heard a girl went to a concert w no earplugs n she's DEAF now, totally, forever, from one gig | Loud noise can damage hearing, so wear ear protection; totally deaf from one gig is likely blown up | One concert always makes a person deaf forever | Loud music never harms the ears at all | Only adults' ears are harmed by loud music
test: the kids are calling Priya "four eyes" since she got glasses, it's going round the whole year | Do not join in; many people wear glasses, and an adult should be told about the name-calling | Join in, glasses are a funny thing to have and everyone says it, so she must be used to it by now | Join in quietly, so she does not hear it | Tell her to stop wearing the glasses instead
exam: Which of the following measures is appropriate when a pupil is unable to read the writing upon the classroom board? | The pupil informs a teacher and a parent, since an eye test may be required | The pupil moves to the back of the room in order to see the board as a whole | The pupil copies a neighbour's work and does not mention the difficulty | The pupil borrows a classmate's spectacles for the remainder of the lesson
exam: Ascertain the minimum daily duration of physical activity recommended for children. | At least one hour per day | At least ten minutes per week | At least five hours per day | No activity is recommended at all
exam: In respect of a sprained ankle, which of the following procedures is consistent with the established facts? | The ankle is rested, cooled with a cold pack and raised, and an adult is informed | The ankle is exercised vigorously at once in order that it should not stiffen | The ankle is placed in hot water for an hour so as to loosen the sprained parts | The ankle is ignored entirely, since sprains invariably heal within minutes
exam: Identify the manner in which one may best assist a deaf or hard-of-hearing classmate to understand speech. | Facing the classmate and speaking clearly without shouting, so as to permit lip-reading | Shouting directly into the classmate's ear from behind, so that the sound is loudest | Removing the classmate's hearing aid in order to test whether it is really required | Speaking with the face turned away, so that the classmate is obliged to listen harder
